The 40 dB design point requires a 9.2 cm thick shell at maximum ferrofluid H-field — too thick for a comfortable wearable. The practical Defender shell uses a thinner Yukawa screen (≈ 3 cm) for 28 dB attenuation, combined with a Dirichlet outer boundary (high-loss-tangent outer layer) providing additional reflective shielding. Combined SE_ψ target: > 35 dB.

== Dirichlet Boundary Mechanism ==

A material with very high ψ loss tangent (<math>\tan\delta_\psi \gg 1</math>) approximates a Dirichlet boundary — incident ψ is absorbed at the interface rather than transmitted. The reflection coefficient at a perfect Dirichlet boundary:

:<math>\Gamma = -1 \qquad (|\Gamma|^2 = 1)</math>

All incident ψ power is reflected. In practice, a finite-loss-tangent material provides partial reflection and partial absorption. The combined Defender mechanism:

# '''Inner high-m_eff layer:''' Yukawa attenuation of transmitted ψ.
# '''Outer high-tan δ layer:''' Dirichlet-approximating reflective boundary blocking incoming ψ.
# '''Between them:''' the operator's protected ψ environment at design coherence length 20 cm ([[Psi Stabilizer]] mode).

== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

The Meissner analogue: In a superconductor, the London penetration depth <math>\lambda_L</math> is the shielding length for magnetic fields — analogous to <math>L_\psi</math> for ψ. A perfect superconductor has complete Meissner expulsion of B. The psionic analogue would be a material with <math>m_{\rm eff} \to \infty</math> (<math>L_\psi \to 0</math>) providing complete ψ expulsion. No room-temperature material achieves this — but high-H ferrofluid saturation at cryogenic temperatures could approach it (speculative Track B).

